{
  "summary": "Refactors output descriptor parsing and BIP32 keypath handling so multipath descriptors are represented as a single Descriptor object instead of being immediately split into multiple descriptors. Replaces raw integer derivation vectors with a dedicated KeyPath class composed of KeyPathElement objects that capture multipath branches and original hardening characters. Updates Parse to return a single Descriptor pointer, adding GetMultipathExpansion and IsMultipath helpers for subsystems that require individual paths.",
  "problem": "Multipath descriptor syntax was treated purely as parse-time syntactic sugar that expanded into a vector of separate descriptors, discarding the multipath specifiers. Consequently, the original descriptor string could not be preserved or returned by RPCs such as getdescriptorinfo.",
  "evidence": [
    "PR description: 'Once we finish parsing such a string, the multipath elements are lost as the result is the multiple distinct descriptors that the multipath is a shorthand for. This means that we are unable to return the multipath string for such descriptors.'",
    "test/functional/rpc_getdescriptorinfo.py: updates getdescriptorinfo assertions so the returned 'descriptor' field maintains the multipath descriptor rather than falling back to the first expanded descriptor."
  ],
  "dependencies": {
    "depends_on": [],
    "enables": [
      "#36443",
      "#36444"
    ]
  },
  "scope_notes": "Touches derivation path representations widely across descriptor parsing, BIP32 utilities, wallet storage/migration, and RPC callers. Replaces std::vector<uint32_t> derivation paths in KeyOriginInfo with the new KeyPath class.",
  "changed_since_previous": "No changes to the commits or implementation approach; diff details are now available.",
  "card": "Represents multipath descriptors natively in a single Descriptor object rather than immediately expanding them during parsing. Previously, multipath syntax was discarded upon parsing as syntactic sugar, preventing RPCs from returning or round-tripping the original multipath descriptor string. Derivation path parsing is refactored into KeyPath and KeyPathElement classes to retain multipath steps and hardening formats, while callers requiring individual paths use GetMultipathExpansion. This work forms the base for #36443 and #36444.",
